# POD Combat — System Reference

Runtime contracts for the combat minigame. Code comments cite the
top-level sections (§1-§11). The execution phases within §2 have a separate
local numbering (§0-§17).

Start with the architecture overview, then use the section cited by the code.

---

## Architecture overview

POD Combat is a turn-based duel between two teams. `pod_combat_max_team_size`
(default 50) caps each team to limit runaway seating calls. Consider turn-order
cost and GUI layout before raising it.

Each combatant has a `combat_team` (`flag:player` or `flag:enemy`) and a
`team_position` (0 = leader). The standard builder seats the PC at player/0
and the primary opponent at enemy/0. Human control is assigned separately (§1).

- **Fight state** lives on a `POD_combat_fight` story, reached through a
  character's `pod_combat_session`. It holds the round, current actor, AP,
  roster, results, and slot pointers, including `pod_combat_pc_slot` and
  `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot`. The combat host receives the duel events;
  it does not own the fight story.
- **Per-combatant state** lives on one `POD_combat_slot` story per seated
  combatant: morale, initiative, hit tiers, stances, reactions, and DoTs.
  These stories last until combat cleanup.
- **Charon** (`character:POD_wraith_01`) owns the fight, slot, move-catalogue
  (`POD_combat_move`), and log-entry (`POD_combat_log_entry`) stories. This
  off-map owner keeps them accessible independently of participant liveness.
- **The current-pair workspace** holds `pod_combat_actor_*` and
  `pod_combat_target_*` on the fight story. It loads at turn setup and target
  changes, then saves after each action. Saving writes back to the slots
  without clearing the pair. PC and AI moves use the same workspace.

HP remains authoritative on the character's PoD wound track. Slot wound bars
are display mirrors, refreshed when HP changes. Prowess and soak are sampled
from the character; soak tiers also live there for PoD's wound calls (§1).

**Hit chance** has a prowess-derived pair base and a tier/stance-derived
live value. With shipped defaults:

```
pair_base = clamp(60 + (own_prowess − target_prowess) / 2, 20, 95)
live_hit  = clamp(pair_base + signed_hit_tiers + stealth_bonus, 20, 95)
```

Script values and debug tuning overrides control the base, clamps, tier
magnitudes, and stealth bonus. A conditional `bonus_effect = hit` can add one further,
re-clamped bonus for the current move only. Slot baselines are seeded
against the opposing leader at initialization; whenever a target is
loaded, both sides are recalculated against the actual actor-target
pair.

---

## §1 Variable namespace

### Where state lives

The owner determines each field's scope and lifetime.

| Owner | Scope it lives on | Lifetime | Representative variables |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Global catalogue/tuning** | global variable or global variable list | save-persistent; catalogue rebuilt on version mismatch, tuning overrides persist until reset | `POD_combat_moves`, `POD_combat_version`, `global_var:pod_combat_tune_*` |
| **Move definition** | a `POD_combat_move` story on Charon | catalogue initialization/reload → next reload | `skill_key`, `targeting`, `ap_cost`, capability flags, payload and rider fields |
| **Host** | combat host (normally the PC character) | field-dependent; event recipient for this duel | `pod_combat.9995` recipient, character modifiers/flags, resource costs, and character-side bridges listed below |
| **Slot** | a `POD_combat_slot` story (one per combatant, on Charon) | slot creation → combat cleanup | `combat_team`, `team_position`, `slot_char`, `slot_alive`, `slot_morale`, `slot_hit_chance`, stance + hit tier vars |
| **Workspace (current pair)** | the `POD_combat_fight` story | actor/target load → replacement load or combat cleanup; saved after each action without being invalidated | `pod_combat_actor_*`, `pod_combat_target_*`, `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` |
| **Character** | the participant character | field-dependent: PC configuration persists; AI loadout/history and combat flags are per fight | persistent PC `pod_combat_loadout` / `pod_combat_manual_step_pref`; AI `pod_combat_ai_last_move` / `_prev_move` / `_prev2_move`; character-side `pod_combat_tier_soak_*`; character flag `pod_combat_nonfatal_active` |
| **Fight** | a `POD_combat_fight` story on Charon, reached through `pod_combat_session` | duel launch → combat cleanup or host-death release | `ctx_host`, `ctx_layout`, principals, rule flags, follow-up ID, lifecycle/counter/outcome vars, roster and slot pointers, turn order, workspace, targeting, AI scratch, weapon profile, reinforcement/summon registries, and resolved-action/log state |
| **Log entry** | a `POD_combat_log_entry` story (one per row, on Charon) | row push → cap trim or combat cleanup | `entry.var:outcome`, `entry.var:attack_result`, `entry.var:damage`, `entry.var:round`, and the move-context fields combat-log customizable localization reads |

**Authoritative character stats are not stored on the slot.** HP and
max HP come from the PoD wound track. Prowess and soak are sampled from
the character when a workspace side loads; PoD evaluates soak again in
the actual wound call. The slot-scoped wound script values are:

| Script value | Returns |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_slot_max_hp_value` | wound track length (max HP) |
| `pod_combat_slot_total_damage_sv` | total wound boxes filled |
| `pod_combat_slot_agg_damage_sv` | aggravated wound boxes filled |
| `pod_combat_slot_hp_urgency_value` | AI heal urgency derived from the live damaged fraction |

There is no `pod_combat_slot_hp_value`: current HP is `max_hp − total_damage`.
The total/agg/max-HP slot mirrors serve only the GUI.

**The workspace holds the loaded pair.** `pod_combat_save_workspace_effect`
writes it back without clearing it. A new actor or target load replaces the
corresponding side. Read other combatants from their slots. Direct writes to a
loaded slot must also update its workspace side or the next save can overwrite
them.

### Where state lives, and what stays on a character

The Charon-owned fight story remains accessible after host death and from
other participants or controllers. Dead character variables can be read but
not written, so character-owned state would prevent complete teardown (§8).

Characters retain:

- **Character-required state:** event recipients, modifiers, flags, resource
  costs, live HP/prowess/soak, and `pod_combat_tier_soak_*` for
  `POD_soak_chance`. PoD's wound call also reads `pod_combat_weapon_attack`
  and `pod_combat_weapon_soak_pen` from the attacker. The latter is distinct
  from the fight-owned `pod_combat_weapon_soak_penalty`.
- **Persistent configuration:** `pod_combat_loadout`,
  `pod_combat_unlocked_moves`, `pod_combat_saved_loadout_*`,
  `pod_combat_manual_step_pref`, `pod_combat_config_open`, and editor/filter
  state.
- **Per-fight AI state:** `pod_combat_ai_last_move` / `_prev_move` /
  `_prev2_move`, the focus trio, `_target_pref`, and `_pers_*` personality vars.

`pod_combat_session` points from a character to the fight; `ctx_host` points
back to the event recipient. Move stories hold the AI decision marks
`pod_combat_ai_score`, `pod_combat_ai_admitted`, `pod_combat_ai_usable_now`,
`pod_combat_ai_bonus_met`, and `pod_combat_ai_eff_ap_drain`.

**Guard session dereferences.** In effects, `var:pod_combat_session ?= { ... }`
skips an absent pointer. In triggers, nest the dereference under a
`has_variable` check; sibling triggers do not short-circuit. Tooltip preview
skips `create_story`, so even a mid-fight effect can encounter an unset pointer
while the launch tooltip is built. See §7 for engine constraints.

```
some_trigger = {
	trigger_if = {
		limit = { has_variable = pod_combat_session }
		var:pod_combat_session ?= { has_variable = pod_combat_active }
	}
	trigger_else = { always = no }
}
```

**Scope switches also relocate value expressions.** A character-scoped script
value evaluated inside `var:pod_combat_session` can silently return 0. Hoist
numeric values with `save_scope_value_as` before entering the story, or use
absolute references such as `scope:slot_story.var:X` and
`scope:combat_host.var:Y`. Do not hoist flags through `save_scope_value_as` (§7).

Inside a fight-scope block, `this` is already the fight. It has no
`pod_combat_session`; a nested dereference would skip in an effect or error in
a trigger. Return through `scope:combat_host` when character scope is needed.

### Variable reference

Fields are grouped by purpose below.

#### Core fight state (on the `POD_combat_fight` story)

Use the session-pointer guards described above when entering fight scope.

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_active` | flag: combat is in progress |
| `pod_combat_is_over` | flag: combat is over (re-entry guard) |
| `pod_combat_round` | current round number |
| `pod_combat_current_ap` | AP remaining this turn |
| `pod_combat_current_actor_team` / `_position` | the acting combatant's team flag + position (0 = leader) |
| `pod_combat_player_team_size` | count of seated player positions; incremented as each ally is added |
| `pod_combat_enemy_team_size` | count of seated enemy positions; incremented as each enemy is added |
| `pod_combat_selected_target_team` / `_position` | the slot currently loaded into the target workspace |
| `pod_combat_ai_enemy_finishable` | a finishable enemy exists; the OR of the two per-kind vars below |
| `pod_combat_ai_enemy_finishable_hp` / `_mor` | per damage kind - each also requires the actor to hold an affordable move of that kind |
| `pod_combat_ai_cur_target_finishable` | the CURRENT target is itself finishable; the kill-snap's gate |
| `pod_combat_ai_can_dmg_hp` / `_mor` | the actor can deal that kind of damage this turn; gates the kill-priority arms |
| `pod_combat_pc_slot` | pointer to the player/0 (PC) slot story |
| `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot` | pointer to the enemy/0 (primary opponent) slot story |
| `pod_combat_active_control_slot` | most recently handed-off human slot; retained through the AI phase for target displays |
| `pod_combat_control_turn_live` | presence: a human-controlled turn is handed off and uncommitted |
| `pod_combat_active_slots` | variable list of every filled slot story; iterated by every dispatch loop |
| `pod_combat_summoned_chars` | variable list of mid-fight summons, disappeared at combat end |

General roster searches iterate `pod_combat_active_slots` and match
`combat_team` + `team_position`.

Every participant carries the character flag `pod_combat_nonfatal_active` in
every fatality mode. It suppresses PoD's inline wound-death check until the
combat-end writeback, where fatal modes resolve deaths in one pass (§8).

#### Per-slot stats (on each `POD_combat_slot` story)

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `combat_team` | flag (`flag:player` / `flag:enemy`): the slot's team, set at creation |
| `team_position` | int 0+: position within the team (0 = leader), set at creation |
| `slot_char` | character ref occupying the slot |
| `slot_alive` | 1 while in play, 0 when KO'd |
| `slot_mirror_total` / `slot_mirror_agg` / `slot_mirror_maxhp` | Wound-bar display only: filled boxes, aggravated boxes and capacity |
| `slot_mirror_raw_sup` / `slot_mirror_raw_agg` | Raw character wound counters at the last mirror write; absent counters sampled as zero, without rounding or clamping |
| `slot_mirror_alive` | Character liveness at the last mirror write (1 living, 0 dead/missing); independent of morale KO and `slot_alive` |
| `slot_morale` | current Morale |
| `slot_hit_chance` | live hit chance (20–95) |
| `slot_base_hit_chance` | hit chance before any tier effects |
| `slot_initiative_base` | the combatant's rolled initiative base, rolled once per fight (per round when initiative reroll is enabled) |
| `slot_initiative` | effective initiative this round: `slot_initiative_base` + any folded `slot_init_bonus` |
| `slot_init_bonus` | next-round initiative modifier |
| `slot_ap_drain` | pending AP drain, consumed after the victim's next-turn AP budget is seeded; subtracts from that budget and floors at 0 |
| `slot_energy_used` | counter feeding AI energy-attrition weighting |
| `slot_turn_order` | turn rank within the round (1 = first) |
| `slot_has_acted` | 1 once the slot has acted this round; reset each round start |
| `slot_dot_amount` / `slot_dot_rounds` | active burn: damage per round-end tick / rounds remaining |
| `slot_dot_kind` | literal flag typing the burn: `flag:morale`, `flag:aggravated`, an elemental class, or `flag:duel` (superficial) |
| `slot_dot_source` | character ref of the burn's caster; the round-end tick credits it as the wound attacker if still alive, else self-attributes the tick to the victim |
| `slot_controlled` | mind-control marker: the slot's next AI turn serves the other side |
| `slot_human_controller` | human controller character; presence selects human turn handling. `slot_controlled` instead marks mind control |
| `slot_target_team` / `_position` | this slot's standing target: team + position. Survives its own AI phase; auto-retargets when the target is KO'd |
| `slot_target_char` | character ref of that target, read by the top-row "Target: X" display |

#### Who controls a turn

`slot_human_controller` assigns human control independently of team or
position. The standard builder assigns the PC slot at finalize; explicit
controller assignment supports additional slots.

- **Turn engine:** `pod_combat_current_actor_is_controlled_trigger` searches
  the roster for the current actor and tests the marker. Use it at turn-advance
  decision points, not in frequently evaluated GUI bindings.
- **GUI:** `pod_combat_is_player_turn_trigger` and
  `pod_combat_can_advance_trigger` read `pod_combat_control_turn_live`.
  `pod_combat_advance_pending_value` controls the End Turn / Advance swap.
  `pod_combat_is_my_turn_trigger` checks that the active slot belongs to the
  clicking client. `pod_combat_my_control_slot` keeps that client's slot
  available for display.

`pod_combat_setup_pc_turn_effect` raises the latch and updates
`pod_combat_active_control_slot`. `pod_combat_process_group_turns_effect`
clears the latch at commit; `pod_combat_execute_ai_turn_effect` also clears it
on entry. The slot pointer remains available for display during AI turns.

#### Per-slot stance flags (on the slot story)

All persist across rounds; cleared only when consumed or at combat end.

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `slot_is_guarding` | Guard stance active |
| `slot_is_dodging` | Dodge stance active |
| `slot_is_stealthed` | Stealth active (adds `pod_combat_stealth_hit_bonus`, default +30, to hit chance) |
| `slot_retaliate_hit` / `slot_retaliate_miss` / `slot_retaliate_any` | retaliation damage if hit / if missed / on any resolved attack |
| `slot_retaliate_{hit,miss,any}_kind` | flag typing the counter: `flag:morale`, `flag:aggravated`, or an elemental class; unset = plain |
| `slot_retaliate_{hit,miss,any}_kind_from_weapon` | weapon- or `change_class`-derived kind; enables the fire-time immunity fallback |
| `slot_retaliate_{hit,miss,any}_morale` | presence companion to a `morale` kind (drives the slot-card status tooltip) |

#### Per-slot spirit-damage capability (on the slot story)

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `slot_incorporeal_spirit` | combatant is incorporeal; physical attacks no-op unless the attacker can damage spirits |
| `slot_can_damage_spirit` | combatant can land physical hits on incorporeal targets |

Both are sampled from trait/perk/artifact triggers at slot initialization.
The combat system does not refresh them during the fight.

#### Tier variables

Tier = 1 (small, ±10), 2 (medium, ±20), 3 (large, ±30). See **Tier system**.

Hit tiers live on the slot story; live hit chance is rebuilt from base plus the
sum of active tier magnitudes:

```
slot_hit_{direction}_{duration}_tier
  direction: buff | debuff
  duration:  round (cleared at the holder's turn boundary) | combat (lasts the fight)
```

Soak tiers live on the COMBATANT, not the slot, as
`pod_combat_tier_soak_{direction}_{duration}`. They are read directly by
`POD_soak_chance`.

The eight magnitude script values
(`pod_combat_slot_{stat}_{direction}_{duration}_mag_sv`) return the
matching tier's magnitude (small/medium/large) or 0 - the four hit
variants read the slot vars, the four soak variants read the occupant's
bridge vars.
`pod_combat_slot_hit_net_mod_value` sums the four hit mags signed (buffs
− debuffs) for the NPC slot-card display.

#### Workspace mirror (transient, on the fight story)

The workspace mirrors the slot fields for whoever is acting
(`pod_combat_actor_*`) and whoever is targeted (`pod_combat_target_*`).

| Variable | Mirrors |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*` | the matching `slot_*` field on the acting / targeted slot (morale, hit_chance, char, energy_used, stance flags, init_bonus, ap_drain, all retaliation vars, the 4 hit tier vars, both spirit flags) |
| `pod_combat_actor_soak` / `_prowess` (and target twins) | NOT mirrors: live values stashed off the combatant (see below) |
| `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` | the active attacker's hit chance for this attack |

Both sides use `pod_combat_load_side_from_story_helper` and
`pod_combat_save_side_to_story_helper` (`SIDE = actor | target`).
`pod_combat_load_target_from_story_effect` also recalculates hit chance for
both sides of the new pair. Add mirrored fields to both scripted effects (§11).
Fight-story `end_story` removes the workspace; character cleanup needs no
workspace `remove_variable` calls.

**Soak and prowess are sampled values.**
`pod_combat_stash_side_live_stats_effect` reads `POD_soak_chance` and `prowess`
from the character into `pod_combat_$SIDE$_soak` / `_prowess` at load and after
soak-tier writes. These values are never saved to slots. Sampling avoids
re-evaluating soak for every AI move candidate's `random_list` modifiers.

**The spirit flags are load-only.** `slot_can_damage_spirit` and
`slot_incorporeal_spirit` copy to the workspace but are not saved back. A new
mechanic that changes them mid-fight must update both the slot and workspace.

Two workspace-scoped script values read the actor's character
directly: `pod_combat_actor_hp_value` (empty wound boxes = HP remaining)
and `pod_combat_actor_max_hp_value` (track length). They gate on
`pod_combat_actor_char` being set and alive.

#### Bonus-clause transients (on the fight story)

The bonus-clause walk stages these in execution phase §2b. They are cleared
at §17 and unconditionally at the start of the next §2b. Each apply site tests
its transient's presence; an omitted clear can apply a stale bonus.

| Variable | Kind | Consumed by |
|---|---|---|
| `pod_combat_bonus_damage` | magnitude | §6 damage adder |
| `pod_combat_bonus_typed_damage_<class>` | magnitude | §6 separately routed typed-damage packet, one bucket per damage class |
| `pod_combat_bonus_hit` | magnitude | §5d hit bump (reverted at §17) |
| `pod_combat_bonus_morale_damage` | magnitude | §7, folded into the base deal call |
| `pod_combat_bonus_self_heal` / `_heal_target` | magnitude | §9b |
| `pod_combat_bonus_self_morale_heal` | magnitude | §9b |
| `pod_combat_bonus_energy_gain` / `_energy_drain` | magnitude | §9b; the unconditional `energy_gain` column folds into the gain transient there, so one pool write carries column and clauses alike |
| `pod_combat_bonus_dot` / `_morale_dot` | magnitude | §13b-bonus / §13b-bonus-morale |
| `pod_combat_bonus_ap_refund` / `_ap_drain` | magnitude | §14 |
| `pod_combat_bonus_retaliate_boost` | magnitude | §13 arming |
| `pod_combat_bonus_hit_buff_round` / `_hit_debuff_round` | tier | §5c tier grant (one round) |
| `pod_combat_bonus_soak_buff_round` / `_soak_debuff_round` | tier | §5c tier grant (one round) |
| `pod_combat_bonus_hit_buff_combat` / `_hit_debuff_combat` | tier | §5c tier grant (rest of combat) |
| `pod_combat_bonus_soak_buff_combat` / `_soak_debuff_combat` | tier | §5c tier grant (rest of combat) |
| `pod_combat_bonus_aggravated` | presence | §6 (`change_type` keeps its historical name) |
| `pod_combat_bonus_stance_guard` / `_stance_dodge` / `_stance_stealth` | presence | §12 |
| `pod_combat_bonus_uses_weapon` | presence | `pod_combat_move_swings_weapon_trigger` |
| `pod_combat_bonus_class` | flag | §6 / counter kind / DoT kind (the `change_class` element) |

Magnitude bonuses add; typed damage adds separately into twelve class-specific
`pod_combat_bonus_typed_damage_<class>` variables, each applied as its own
wound packet in §6. The magnitude clear lists therefore contain twenty-four
literal names. Tier bonuses take the maximum, then
`pod_combat_upgrade_tier_from_bonus_effect` upgrades the recipient's matching
tier without lowering an existing one. Round and combat durations use
different destination variables; both transients clear at §17.

**Known limitation:** clause-granted tiers produce no combat-log segment.
`pod_combat_push_log_effect`, the `hit_buff_round_tier` gate in
`POD_combat_log_custom_loc.txt`, and the tier display values read the move's
static columns. A clause changes the fight transient and recipient tier,
leaving those columns unset. Supporting the log requires recording effective
tiers on the log entry at push time, then reading them in the loc gates and
`pod_combat_values.txt` tier values. Changing only the gate would print an
empty tier.

Presence and class fields are set once. `pod_combat_bonus_class` uses literal
`value = flag:X` writes. Typed packets pass their class as a macro literal,
and log capture writes `flag:$CLASS$`; flag comparisons depend on preserving
the type (§7).

#### Result / display (on the fight story)

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_winner` / `pod_combat_loser` | character ref: who won / lost |
| `pod_combat_last_damage` | last HP damage dealt |
| `pod_combat_last_heal_amount` | last HP heal applied |
| `pod_combat_last_morale_damage_amount` / `_last_morale_heal_amount` | last morale damage / heal |
| `pod_combat_last_self_damage` | post-soak recoil damage applied to the actor |
| `pod_combat_last_energy_gain` / `_last_energy_drain` | requested amount when restore/drain fired; gain includes the column, met clauses, and feeding weapon (§9c). Splat energy effects do not export the pool-capped applied delta |
| `pod_combat_last_ap_refund` | AP returned by a fired `ap_refund` bonus (§14); used by the log |
| `pod_combat_last_ap_drain` | base plus fired bonus drain applied to the target workspace (§14); written only for positive totals inside the control-failure guard. The log uses this total |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_damage` | last retaliation damage |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_agg` / `_last_retal_morale` | actual-result markers for the counter: it landed aggravated (PoD's per-victim classification) / it eroded morale instead of HP |
| `pod_combat_last_result_agg` | actual-result marker for the main damage line: PoD classified the landed wound as aggravated for this victim (lifted from the damage-routing scripted effect's `scope:result_agg`) — see **Result model** |
| `pod_combat_last_soaked_count` | points absorbed by PoD's per-point soak roll on the last damage write (lifted from the damage-routing scripted effect's `scope:soaked_count`); absent = none. Drives the damage line's partial-soak note — see **Result model** |
| `pod_combat_last_outcome` | optional top-level fight outcome flag (`flag:attack` / `heal` / `morale_damage` / `morale_healed`); a pure utility move leaves it absent and `push_log` creates `entry.var:outcome = flag:utility` — see **Result model** |
| `pod_combat_last_attack_result` | inner attack resolution (`flag:hp_damage` / `soaked` / `immune` / `missed` / `dodged` / `stealthed` / `guard_blocked`); only meaningful when outcome = attack |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_fired` | overlay flag: a retaliation fired on this row |
| `pod_combat_aoe_any_hp_damage` | AoE latch: any target took HP damage this burst |

`pod_combat_force_agg` instead lives on the acting character. It is set and
removed around an elemental dispatch when `change_type` forces aggravated
classification. `POD_damage_type_is_aggravated` reads it through
`POD_wound_attacker_forces_agg_trigger`, which returns no without
`scope:wound_attacker`. See §4, **Damage pipeline**.

#### AI move-history (on the acting AI character, not the fight story)

Each AI pick shifts these `POD_combat_move` story references on the acting
character. Anti-repetition penalties compare them by scope equality. They and
the AI's `pod_combat_loadout` clear at slot initialization and combat cleanup.

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_ai_last_move` | most recent move |
| `pod_combat_ai_prev_move` | second-most-recent pick |
| `pod_combat_ai_prev2_move` | third-most-recent pick |

Shift order: `prev2 ← prev`, `prev ←
last`, `last ← chosen`.

