## §2 Execution phases - `POD_execute_combat_move`

`POD_execute_combat_move` (`pod_combat_move_effects.txt`) executes every
move by reading its `POD_combat_move` story, passed as `$STORY$`. Move
definitions supply damage, healing, tiers, stances, reactions and summons;
there is no per-move execution code.

Call in **combat-host scope**. The pipeline reaches fight state through
`scope:combat_host.var:pod_combat_session`. Before calling, load the
`pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*` workspace aliases on the
**fight story**:

- **actor**: the PC or AI combatant taking the action.
- **target**: the current single target, replaced during AoE iteration or
  AI target selection.

The callers are the `pod_combat_execute_move` scripted GUI and
`pod_combat_ai_select_and_execute_move_effect`.

### Phases

Execution follows this order. Most payload phases require a move-story
field; setup, result logging and teardown run under their own gates.
See **Phase ordering rules** before reordering phases.

| Phase | What it does |
|---|---|
| §0 | Save the host as `scope:combat_host`, also used to re-enter host scope during AoE iteration. |
| §1 | Spend `ap_cost` AP through `pod_combat_spend_ap_effect`. |
| §1b | For `once_per`, append the move story to the actor slot's round- or combat-duration used list. Usability rejects reuse; initiative setup clears the round list. |
| §2 | Clear result axes through `pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect`, set `pod_combat_last_damage = 0`, and clear the pure-control delivery-failure latch. An absent `pod_combat_last_*` field means no outcome yet. |
| §2b | **Evaluate bonus clauses once**, in host scope, against `pod_combat_actor_char`. Clear all bonus transients unconditionally before walking `bonus_clauses`, including on clause-less moves. Met clauses sum magnitudes by effect and typed damage by class; tiers take the maximum; stance/weapon/aggravation bonuses set presence fields; `change_class` sets `pod_combat_bonus_class`. Later phases each apply their accumulated effect once. This preserves energy rounding and prevents DoT seeds or damage adders from overwriting earlier clauses. `uses_weapon` must be resolved before §2c. Gate-mode conditions are checked by usability before execution; a gate move may also carry bonuses. See **Bonus-clause transients** in §1 for the complete field set. |
| §2c | **Weapon profile.** `pod_combat_move_swings_weapon_trigger` accepts static or condition-granted `uses_weapon`. Resolve the eligible `primary_armament` and set the six fight fields: `pod_combat_weapon_class`, `_bonus_damage`, `_undodgeable`, `_heal_on_damage`, `_energy_on_damage`, `_soak_penalty`. Clear the profile on every action before checking eligibility. Retype applies only over a superficial/default move class. §17 and `pod_combat_cleanup_effect` also clear it. See **Weapon artifact overrides**. |
| §3 | Pay `energy_cost` through `POD_splat_agnostic_energy_cost` **in actor-character scope**, then increment AI energy attrition. Calling from host scope would charge the PC for AI casts. |
| §4 | On `pod_combat_current_actor_is_controlled_trigger` turns, pay `gold_cost` / `stress_cost` / `piety_cost` / `prestige_cost` and apply `steal_gold` / `gain_dread`. Gold, piety and prestige charge the driver, matching affordability checks; stress and gains affect the acting body. AI turns are free. Practice pays costs but suppresses theft and Dread gain. |
| §5 | Strip target stances before damage: `bypass_dodge` removes dodge; `bypass_stealth` also removes the current stealth hit bonus; `remove_guard` clears guard. A `requires_control_contact` guard strip waits until §5b2. |
| §5b | For `team_enemy` / `team_all`, broadcast debuff tiers and stance strips before per-target damage through `pod_combat_apply_team_enemy_debuffs_effect` / `pod_combat_apply_team_all_debuffs_effect`. `team_all` also applies the caster's tier share through `pod_combat_apply_team_all_self_debuffs_effect`. |
| §5b2 | **Pure-control delivery.** `requires_control_contact` sends a single-enemy move with no HP/morale payload through the contact resolver. Failure suppresses guard strip, tiers, mind control, AP drain and initiative debuff. Costs and once-per usage remain spent; actor stealth is consumed; the log reports the miss/block without undelivered payloads. |
| §5c | **Tier buffs/debuffs.** Buff the actor, or the selected target for `single_ally`; debuff the target. Only first applications and strictly higher tiers write. Failed §5b2 delivery suppresses all declared tiers. Recalculate beneficiary hit chance, write character soak tiers, and refresh sampled soak. See **Tier system**. |
| §5d | Add the accumulated `hit` bonus to `pod_combat_current_hit_chance`, capped at `pod_combat_hit_chance_max`. §17 restores the ordinary tier+stealth value. |
| §6 | **HP damage.** Dispatch `single_enemy`, `team_enemy` or `team_all`; resolve contact once per target. Apply the main packet (base + untyped bonus + weapon bonus), then each filled typed-damage bucket in class-table order. Each typed packet has its own class, immunity, soak and result snapshot. Remove actor force-aggravated/weapon markers before the first typed packet. Gameplay uses aggregate results; the log retains primary and up to three typed snapshots. The separately applied `team_all` caster share receives no typed, untyped or weapon bonus. See **Damage pipeline**. |
| §7 | Deal morale damage through `pod_combat_deal_morale_damage_effect` or its team variant. Auto-hit; ignores guard and soak; stealth/dodge still block. `morale_damage_condition` gates delivery. Fold the bonus into the base call, including team broadcasts, for one stance consumption and one combined amount. |
| §8 | Apply actor recoil through `pod_combat_deal_hp_damage_bypass_guard_effect`, `REASON = duel`, gated by `self_damage_condition`. PoD controls classification and soak. Store the post-soak amount in `pod_combat_last_self_damage`; preserve the attack's result axes. |
| §9 | Heal HP, gated by `heal_condition`, after §6. `heal_type = aggravated` also heals aggravated boxes; otherwise cap at `max_hp − agg_damage`. Route to actor, `single_ally`, `single_pc` or `team_ally`. There is no `team_all` broadcast: its runtime fallback heals only the caster, and authored `team_all` moves must not declare healing. Drain healing requires explicit `on_hp_damage`. This gate reads aggregate damage, so a successful typed packet qualifies even if the main packet was soaked or immune. |
| §9b | Deliver independent bonuses: `self_heal` restores actor superficial HP; `heal_target` restores target superficial HP; `self_morale_heal` restores actor morale; `energy_gain` combines base and bonus into one actor-pool write; `energy_drain` drains the target outside practice. Store fired energy amounts for logging; the morale primitive already writes `pod_combat_last_morale_heal_amount`. |
| §9c | **Weapon recovery.** Heal/energy overrides use `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_hp_damage_trigger`, including the AoE `pod_combat_aoe_any_hp_damage` latch. They fire once per action; full soak gives no recovery. Use `pod_combat_restore_hp_superficial_effect` and actor-scoped `POD_splat_agnostic_energy_gain`. Add energy to the §9b log amount. As with §9b, even a zero heal can produce the AoE wrap-up heal row; energy logs the requested amount, not the pool-capped delta. |
| §10 | Apply `morale_heal` through `pod_combat_restore_morale_effect`, targeting actor, `single_ally`, `single_pc` or `team_ally`. No rider condition. |
| §12 | Apply `set_guard`, `dodge` and `stealth` idempotently to the selected `single_ally` target, otherwise to the actor. Hostile `remove_guard` belongs to §5/§5b2. |
| §12b | For `team_ally`, broadcast move-declared tiers, stance flags and the soak bridge through `pod_combat_apply_team_ally_buffs_effect`. Condition-granted `stance_*` bonuses remain actor-only. |
| §12b-marker | Remove `remove_marker` from the actor (`remove_marker_target = user`) or loaded target (`target`, single-enemy/single-ally only). Self/team moves have no reliable broadcast target for this operation. Only combat-owned `MARKER_MODIFIERS` keys may be removed. Run after condition evaluation and before marker application so a move can consume one mark and leave another. |
| §12c | Apply `marker_modifier` to the actor for 30 days through a literal modifier switch. Markers have no numeric effects and are read by later moves' `has_modifier` conditions. Applying after §2b prevents a move from satisfying its own condition. See §11 **Adding a marker character modifier**. |
| §13 | Arm actor retaliation: `retaliate_if_hit` (contact), `retaliate_if_missed` (evasion), or `retaliate_if_attacked` (any resolved attack). Stage `retaliate_damage → hp_damage → 1` in `pod_combat_retal_arm_candidate`, then add `retaliate_boost`. Each family compares and writes this same amount; only a stronger charge replaces the family, and ties preserve kind. Kind precedence: morale > `retaliate_agg` > move element > granted `change_class` element > weapon retype > plain. Weapon/conversion kinds carry `_kind_from_weapon` and fall back at fire time if `pod_combat_stamped_type_is_wasted_trigger` finds an immune victim. |
| §13b | `pod_combat_seed_dot_on_slot_effect` writes target `slot_dot_amount/rounds/kind/source`; source is the actor for kill credit. Reject miss-class results and KO targets. Guard block, full soak and immunity still permit application, as does a landed morale DoT without an HP `attack_result`. Kind starts from the move class/default `duel`, with weapon/conversion overrides and `dot_morale` taking precedence. Reapplication replaces the slot's DoT. Log amount, rounds and HP/morale track; future HP ticks bypass per-point soak but retain immunity, Clay Man, weakness multipliers/cap and PoD classification. See **Combat log** / **Turn loop**. |
| §13b-bonus | A met `dot` clause seeds the same slot using summed `pod_combat_bonus_dot` and `pod_combat_bonus_dot_rounds` (2). Use the §13b miss/KO gate. Before slot iteration, save `pod_combat_dot_is_morale = 0`; the DoT-seeding scripted effect reads this scope value independently of other bonuses. |
| §13b-bonus-morale | Use the same seed path with `pod_combat_bonus_morale_dot`, saving `pod_combat_dot_is_morale = 1` before iteration. The DoT-seeding scripted effect applies morale after all HP-kind overrides, then the caller logs `dot_applied_morale`. Do not combine `morale_dot` with base DoT: both write the single `slot_dot_*` record, so the later seed replaces the earlier one. |
| §13c | `mind_control` marks the single target `slot_controlled` for one stolen turn. Players are immune; `POD_has_strong_mental_defenses_trigger` blocks unless `can_bypass_mental_defenses_trigger` succeeds. Require successful §5b2 delivery for pure control, or landed morale damage for morale-bearing control. A resisted cast logs resistance. |
| §14 | Apply `ap_gain` and bonus `ap_refund` to current AP. Sum base+bonus `ap_drain`, take-max into the target mirror, and consume it at the target's next turn start. Store fired refund/drain totals as `pod_combat_last_ap_refund` / `_ap_drain` for logging; `pod_combat_clear_per_target_state_effect` clears them. Stack `initiative_debuff` negatively into target initiative bonus for the next round. Failed §5b2 delivery suppresses target drain/debuff. |
| §14b | `summons_combatant` runs its allow-listed creation recipe and seats the result on the friendly or `summon_hostile` side through `pod_combat_summon_combatant_glue_effect`. `controlled_animal` creates a fixed-Prowess physical Wolf Form animal, upgraded to a stronger single-slot swarm by `plague_of_beasts_perk`. The summon acts next round. Running after damage prevents a same-cast AoE hit; running before §15 prevents a summon-turn counter. |
| §15 | Check target retaliation through `pod_combat_check_retaliation_effect` only for moves carrying `hp_damage` with targeting other than `team_enemy` / `team_all`. Pure morale/control and team AoE trigger no reactions. At most one counter fires; a resolved hit/miss counter preserves Always. A spirit-blocked charge can also consume Always (§6). See **Reactions**. |
| §16 | Push the result through `pod_combat_push_log_effect`, subject to the AoE gate below. |
| §17 | If §5d changed hit chance, rebuild the ordinary tier+stealth value **before** clearing `pod_combat_bonus_hit`. Clear all 12 untyped magnitudes, 12 typed buckets, eight tiers, five presence fields and `pod_combat_bonus_class`. Missing clears can replay bonuses on the next action; §2b repeats the clear to handle aborted execution. Also clear the weapon profile and remove any remaining actor `pod_combat_force_agg`. |

### The §16 log gate

Non-AoE moves always push a summary row. AoE iterators in §6/§7/§9/§10
push per-target rows and set `pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows`. §16 skips
the redundant summary unless a later, once-per-action result needs it.

The gate pushes when **any** of these holds:

- `pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows` is absent.
- `pod_combat_last_heal_amount` or `pod_combat_last_morale_heal_amount`
  is present (actor healing).
- `pod_combat_last_retal_fired` is present (counter).
- `pod_combat_last_self_damage` is present (recoil).
- `pod_combat_last_energy_gain` / `_energy_drain` is present (§9b/§9c).
- `pod_combat_last_ap_refund` or `pod_combat_last_ap_drain` is present
  (§14, after per-target rows).

### Phase ordering rules

- **§2b before §2c:** conditional `uses_weapon` must be known before
  weapon-profile setup.
- **§5 / §5b / §5c before §6:** the move's own damage uses its stance
  strips and tier changes.
- **§5b2 before §5c/§12/§13c/§14:** resolve pure-control delivery before
  any gated payload writes.
- **§7 / §8 / §9 after §6:** rider conditions read
  `pod_combat_last_outcome` and `pod_combat_last_attack_result`.
- **§15 after §6:** retaliation uses the current attack result.
- **§16 after all outcomes, before §17:** log complete results before
  clearing transients.
- **§14b after damage/AoE, before §15:** the new summon cannot be hit by
  its summoning action or counter during it.
- **Marker removal/application after §2b, removal before application:**
  evaluate existing marks before consuming or replacing them.

### Damage pipeline

Combat resolves contact: spirit gate, stealth/dodge, hit roll and guard.
PoD's wound system handles the connected packet: soak, splat weaknesses,
classification, wound writes and death checks. Combat does not edit wound
boxes directly.

#### Capability tags decide the pipeline shape

`pod_combat_resolve_contact_effect` reads four optional flags from
`scope:current_move`, authored through scripted effects in the registry:

| Capability flag | Effect when present |
|---|---|
| `checks_spirit_gate` | Requires actor spirit-damage capability against an incorporeal spirit. |
| `requires_hit_roll` | Roll `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` against 100; absent means auto-hit. |
| `checks_guard` | Consume target guard and block the attack. |
| `checks_stance` | Consume stealth, then dodge, for an automatic miss. |

The physical convention sets all four. The supernatural convention sets
only `checks_stance`: auto-hit, bypass guard, reach spirits, but remain
evadable. There is no runtime `attack_type` field. Morale has its own
auto-hit/no-guard/no-soak path and checks target stealth/dodge regardless
of these flags.

Set `requires_control_contact` on a `single_enemy` move with no HP or
morale payload when its control effects must pass the resolver. It
enables delivery checking without changing the capability profile;
without it, pure control/utility is guaranteed.

#### Contact resolution

`pod_combat_resolve_contact_effect` writes resolver-local integer
`scope:contact_result`, distinct from the result-axis flags:

1. **Spirit gate:** blocked capability → `6` (`missed`).
2. **Stance:** `pod_combat_consume_target_stance_effect` consumes stealth
   → `4` (`stealthed`), otherwise dodge → `3` (`dodged`). A target with
   both retains dodge for the next attack.
3. **Hit roll:** if required, weight `random_list` by current hit chance;
   failure → `2` (`missed`). Otherwise continue.
4. **Guard:** `pod_combat_check_guard_effect` consumes checked guard
   → `1` (`guard_blocked`); otherwise `0` (hit).

Every non-hit branch sets result axes and `pod_combat_last_damage = 0`.
Result `0` reaches `pod_combat_apply_primary_damage_payload_effect`, then
each filled typed bucket reaches
`pod_combat_apply_typed_bonus_damage_payload_effect` in class-table order,
reusing the same contact result. A `damage` clause with blank
`bonus_param` adds to the main packet instead.

HP dispatch and pure-control delivery consume actor stealth regardless
of outcome, once per HP AoE action. Morale-only delivery consumes target
stealth/dodge but preserves actor stealth.

For `requires_control_contact`, result `0` permits hostile non-damage
payloads; any other result sets the fight's failure latch. Logging keeps
the outcome and suppresses undelivered tier/strip/AP/initiative segments.

#### The class dispatch — damage class → PoD death reason

The main packet uses `var:damage_type`. `POD_combat_move_set_hp_damage`
defaults it to `superficial`; call `POD_combat_move_set_damage_class`
**afterward** to avoid overwriting an explicit class. The dispatch in
`pod_combat_apply_single_hp_damage_effect` maps it to PoD's `$REASON$`:

| `damage_type` | `death_reason` |
|---|---|
| `superficial` (the default) or unset | `duel` |
| `aggravated` | `duel_aggravated` |
| `fire` | `fire` |
| `cold` | `cold` |
| `lightning` | `lightning` |
| `sun` | `sun` |
| `silver` | `silver` |
| `gold` | `gold` |
| `holy` | `true_faith` |
| `balefire` | `balefire` |
| `poison` | `poison` |
| `cold_iron` | `cold_iron` |

A typed `damage` clause names one of these classes in `bonus_param` and
uses the same mapping for its own packet. Its immunity, weakness, soak
and classification are independent of the main packet. Remove the main
packet's weapon retype, forced-aggravated marker and weapon soak
penetration once, before the first typed packet.

Author at most **three distinct typed classes** per move. Same-class
clauses sum into one packet. Runtime supports twelve buckets, but the log
has only three typed segments. Unlike `change_class` and weapon retype,
typed damage does not test whether conversion would be wasted: an immune
packet logs `Immune` without replacing the move's main damage.

`POD_damage_type_is_aggravated` and `POD_has_immunity_to_damage_type`
(`common/scripted_triggers/POD_health_track_triggers.txt`) determine the
per-victim result. Elements retain their real death reason for immunity,
superficial/aggravated classification and splat multipliers; explicit
`aggravated` uses `duel_aggravated`. In particular, `holy` maps to
`true_faith` because that is PoD's classification key.

`pod_combat_move_class_is_elemental_trigger` identifies the ten elements.
`pod_combat_class_always_aggravated_trigger` groups `aggravated`, `fire`
and `balefire` for AI/log heuristics; it does not replace the per-victim
matrix.

Keep the mapping synchronized with the weapon-retype switch,
`pod_combat_apply_typed_bonus_damage_effect`,
`pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect`,
`pod_combat_deal_dot_tick_from_host_effect` and §2b's typed buckets.
§11 **Adding a new damage class** lists all extension sites.

### Weapon artifact overrides

Static or condition-granted `uses_weapon` applies the actor's eligible
`primary_armament` overrides for one action, subject to
`can_benefit_from_artifact`. Both paths use
`pod_combat_move_swings_weapon_trigger` in §2c and §6, then share the
six-field fight profile. Only damage class may fall back to another
eligible equipped artifact; other profile fields come from the weapon.

| Artifact variable | Effect on a `uses_weapon` move |
|---|---|
| `artifact_weapon_damage_class` (flag) | Retype a superficial/default move to the element, unless the victim is immune (`pod_combat_weapon_retype_is_wasted_trigger`). Immune targets take the move's original damage. Authored elements/aggravated are unchanged. |
| `artifact_weapon_bonus_damage` (int) | Add flat HP damage to every reached target. |
| `artifact_weapon_undodgeable` | Bypass target dodge/stealth without consuming either, as if `checks_stance` were absent. |
| `artifact_weapon_heal_on_damage` (int) | Restore N superficial actor wound boxes after an action that deals HP damage. |
| `artifact_weapon_energy_on_damage` (int) | Restore N splat-agnostic actor energy after an action that deals HP damage. PoD multiplies by `splat_energy_multiplier` (`maximum_splat_energy / 10`) and rounds up. Author even values because preview localization cannot round up; N = 2 gives 1 Blood on a five-point pool and 2 Gnosis on a ten-point pool. |
| `artifact_weapon_soak_penalty` (int) | Reduce the victim's soak chance by N percentage points for this packet, floored at 0. |
| `artifact_deals_aggravated`, or membership in `POD_weapon_artifact_forces_agg_trigger` | Force aggravated classification when the per-victim condition applies. |

**Weapon-only aggravation.** `POD_weapon_artifact_forces_agg_trigger`
(`common/scripted_triggers/POD_weapon_artifact_triggers.txt`) handles
`artifact_deals_aggravated`, `artifact_agg_vs_*` tags and
`blade_of_the_kings`' power-versus-prowess condition. In combat it requires
actor marker `pod_combat_weapon_attack`, set only around a weapon move's
main damage. It therefore excludes non-weapon moves, typed riders, DoT
ticks and recoil.

**Soak penalty ownership.** The fight profile's
`pod_combat_weapon_soak_penalty` is copied to actor-character
`pod_combat_weapon_soak_pen` during §6. PoD's wound call can reach the
attacker, so it subtracts this bridge value from `scope:soak_pct` inside
`POD_increase_wounds_effect`, under a `scope:combat_host` guard and nested
`trigger_if`, then floors the result at 0. No later step clamps it.

Keep the penalty out of `POD_soak_chance`: cards, AI sampling, the sidebar
and maim rolls evaluate that value without an attacker. They should show
base soak. The combat log already measures actual wound deltas and
absorbed points. Give the bridge exactly the same lifetime as
`pod_combat_weapon_attack`; a leaked penalty can affect the attacker's
own soak when a counter fires.

**Energy display.** Costs and gains use separate conversions:

| Surface | Whose resource | Number conversion |
|---|---|---|
| Move card/loadout sheet: listed `energy_cost` | Living retained control-slot combatant in active combat, otherwise reader (`GetPlayer`) | `PODCombatMoveEnergyCost` calls `pod_combat_move_energy_cost_display_value`, which converts the full cost and rounds up. |
| Move card/loadout sheet: base or bonus `energy_gain`, weapon override | Reader (`GetPlayer`) | Localization multiplies by `splat_energy_multiplier`. |
| Combat log gain | Entry actor | `pod_combat_scale_energy_to_splat_effect` stores real pool points. |
| Combat log drain | Entry target | Same scripted effect, scaled for the target. |
| Move card `energy_drain` bonus | Generic | No target exists yet. |

Cost previews use encounter Dharma discounts even outside a duel; actual
spending and affordability require `pod_combat_duel_active` for the discount.
`PODCombatEnergyCostIcon` / `PODCombatEnergyCostWord` select the same character
as the cost script value. The preview shows the full listed cost, before any
vessel contribution.

Gain previews still use `PODEnergySplatWord` / `PODEnergySplatIcon` and direct
localization arithmetic. Unlike cost previews and combat-log conversions,
they do not round up before formatting; even amounts avoid fractional display
mismatches on five-point pools.

**Outside combat.** Applicable overrides use `POD_is_death_by_weapon`
(`common/scripted_triggers/POD_deaths_triggers.txt`), covering weapon
death reasons such as duels, brawls, battle, tournaments and assassination:

| Effect | Outside the minigame |
|---|---|
| `artifact_weapon_damage_class` | Classify as the element only if the victim matches the relevant `POD_weak_to_*` trigger and lacks `POD_has_immunity_to_damage_type`. Otherwise retain the ordinary wound. |
| `artifact_deals_aggravated` (and the named bane list) | Aggravate weapon wounds only. |
| `artifact_weapon_soak_penalty` | Subtract from the packet's soak roll at the same wound-system site. |
| Bonus damage / undodgeable / lifesteal / energy | Minigame only. |

Keep the outside classification branches in `POD_damage_type_is_aggravated`,
not the `POD_is_death_by_*` scripted triggers. Those triggers also feed immunity and
`POD_wound_multiplier_for_vanilla_death_reason` /
`POD_wound_multiplier_from_splat_weaknesses`; extending them would change
ordinary packet magnitudes or let elemental immunity negate the weapon
wound. Outside branches require `NOT = { exists = scope:combat_host }`
because combat dispatch already applies its own classification.

The class, forced-aggravation and soak branches also reject attacker =
victim. `scope:wound_attacker` persists in the saved scope table, and some
non-weapon effects use `REASON = fight`, so death reason alone cannot
exclude stale attackers. A stale third-party attacker remains a known
limitation; removing it requires a freshness marker in PoD's hostile
wound scripted effects. PoD lethal duels already use `duel_aggravated`; the
outside weapon class mainly affects nonfatal duels and `fight` wounds.

**Adding a weapon.** Stamp the applicable override variables in its
creation effect. Runtime, AI and tooltips read these variables; existing
overrides require no artifact-name, GUI or localization entry. Avoid
duplicating generated mechanics in the artifact description. The display
uses `[POD_aggravated_damage|E]` and `$pod_combat_elemmark_*$` icons.

**Conditional banes.** `POD_weapon_artifact_bane_applies_trigger` reads
composable presence tags:

| Tag | Aggravates against |
|---|---|
| `artifact_agg_vs_vampire` | Vampires and abominations. |
| `artifact_agg_vs_werewolf` | Werewolves. |
| `artifact_agg_vs_demon` | Demons. |
| `artifact_agg_vs_fera` | Fera. |
| `artifact_agg_vs_wyrm_tainted` | `POD_is_wyrm_servant_trigger`: `rf_wyrm` religions, Wyrm splat traits and tainted faiths, including Black Spiral Dancers and Viatantabus. |
| `artifact_agg_except_werewolf` | All except werewolves. Supported by the trigger and `PODWeaponOvrAggExceptWerewolf`, but currently unused. |

Each tag emits its own tooltip line; multiple tags compose. Silver uses
`artifact_weapon_damage_class = flag:silver` and PoD's weakness matrix,
not a bane tag. Quiver of Silvered Arrows uses this class from a trinket
slot, which is why class lookup permits an equipped-artifact fallback.
The one named condition, `blade_of_the_kings`, compares
`artifact_power >= victim.prowess` in
`POD_weapon_artifact_forces_agg_trigger`.

**Existing saves are not migrated.** Artifacts retain the variables
stamped when they were created. Changing a creation effect changes future
artifacts, not existing instances; the same named artifact can therefore
behave differently between saves.

**Override text.** `POD_weapon_override_custom_loc.txt` emits one line
per override. Both callers bind `scope:pod_weapon`:
`PODWeaponOverrideSelfText` on artifact surfaces, and `save_scope_as =
pod_weapon` before the move-card tooltip. The artifact tooltip
(`gui/shared/cooltip.gui`) and details window
(`gui/window_artifact_details.gui`) render
`pod_artifact_desc_and_weapon_override` as
`[PODWeaponOverrideSelfText][Artifact.GetDesc]`. `PODWeaponOvrHeader` and
`PODWeaponOvrFooter` use the shared
`POD_weapon_artifact_has_override_trigger` to bracket the lines with
"When wielded:" and spacing.

Keep this composition at widget level. `Artifact.GetDesc` localizes in a
fresh environment without the `Artifact` data context; embedding
`[PODWeaponOverrideSelfText]` in a description key errors and renders
blank.

**Authored versus equipped class.** The move's class display, sheet `Typ`
column, compact damage marks, class filters and AI class scoring use the
shared catalogue's authored class. The log reports the actual per-victim
classification. The card adds a viewer-specific weapon line: generic
"Uses Your Weapon" when unarmed, the weapon's name when armed, or a named
override block when the weapon carries overrides. That block shares the
artifact tooltip customizable localization.

**`change_type`.** The §2b `pod_combat_bonus_aggravated` bonus routes a
non-elemental packet through `duel_aggravated`. An elemental packet must
retain its element for immunity checks, so dispatch temporarily stamps
actor `pod_combat_force_agg`.
`POD_wound_attacker_forces_agg_trigger`, called by
`POD_damage_type_is_aggravated`, reads it through `scope:wound_attacker`
and returns false for an absent attacker. Classification is synchronous:
remove the marker immediately after dispatch; §17 clears it again.

#### Soak and the wound write

`pod_combat_route_to_pod_damage_effect` passes raw `COUNT` to
`POD_increase_wounds_hostile_effect`. PoD applies immunity, per-point soak,
splat weaknesses, damage caps, classification and death checks. For
example, a four-point hit at 50% soak rolls four times, allowing partial
absorption.

The route exports scope values, without stored-variable writes:

- `scope:damage_dealt`: actual wound-box/severity delta, zero if no boxes
  land; fatal-path damage is capped at the victim's pre-hit empty boxes.
- `scope:attack_result_flag`: `flag:hp_damage`, `soaked` or `immune`.
- `scope:result_agg`: classification for this victim and reason, captured
  before the wound call can remove victim state.
- `scope:soaked_count`: absorbed points, captured before wound writes.

For survivors, damage is `max(0, superficial_delta) + max(0,
aggravated_delta)`, so aggravated displacement of superficial boxes does
not appear as soak. Morale spillover uses actual post-soak damage and
`pod_combat_morale_from_hp_ratio`.

`pod_combat_apply_damage_payload_effect` sums absorbed points into
`pod_combat_last_soaked_count` as a fallback. Packet-specific scripted effects preserve
`pod_combat_last_primary_soaked_count` and
`pod_combat_last_typed_bonus_soaked_count_<k>`; `push_log` copies these to
`entry.var:soaked_count` and `entry.var:typed_bonus_soaked_count_<k>`.
`PODCombatLogResultText` and `PODCombatLogSegTypedBonusDamage<k>` each show
their own damage and partial-soak note, or `Soaked` for a full soak.

Soak, PoD wound notifications and morale spillover run per packet. A move
with three typed riders can produce four wound notifications and four
spillover calculations. Their spillover total equals a merged packet only
while the ratio is integral; a fractional ratio rounds down per packet.

#### Wound-value reuse

`pod_combat_route_to_pod_damage_effect` reads canonical superficial and
aggravated boxes into `scope:pre_superficial_wounds` and
`scope:pre_aggravated_wounds` for each packet. It derives
`scope:pre_empty_boxes` as `POD_health_track_length` minus the floored sum
of those two snapshots, matching `POD_empty_damage_boxes` without another
wound-track read. There is no added lower clamp or final floor. After the
wound call, surviving victims still receive independent canonical
superficial and aggravated reads for the damage delta.

`POD_update_wound_trait_xp` captures `POD_health_track_percentage` into
`scope:pod_wound_update_percentage` fresh on every call. The parameterized
`POD_has_wrong_wound_trait_at_percentage` shares the existing threshold
predicate with the retained public `POD_has_wrong_wound_trait`, which still
samples live percentage into temporary `scope:trait_xp` for its callers.
The update effect reuses its captured value for the predicate, all four
threshold checks and the final XP assignment. After zero-wound cleanup
removes both damage variables, it resets the saved percentage to zero.
Trait operations, their order and the final
`force_character_skill_recalculation` remain unchanged.

Saved scope values last through the unbroken event chain; they are not
stack-local. These snapshots must be overwritten on entry and never used
as a cache between actors, packets or events. Wound traits and their XP
currently do not affect health-track-length inputs. Re-audit the reuse if
those inputs, trait operations or synchronous hooks change, especially if
an update could alter the wound state or re-enter before the last read.

#### The spirit gate

The contact resolver blocks `checks_spirit_gate` moves against an
incorporeal spirit when the actor lacks spirit-damage capability, logging
`missed`. PoD's wound system has no equivalent gate.

Retaliation bypasses the resolver, so
`pod_combat_apply_retaliation_effect` checks this separately. It blocks
**every counter kind**, including morale, against an incorporeal attacker
when the retaliator lacks spirit damage.

Auto-resolve uses `pod_combat_player_side_cannot_win_trigger` and its enemy
counterpart: a side facing a living incorporeal spirit needs a
`pod_combat_slot_has_spirit_answer_trigger` match to win. See
**Auto-resolve**. Manual morale attacks bypass the gate and can win by
morale KO, but the auto-resolve verdict does not count them as an answer.

#### The actual result — `scope:result_agg`

Authored class and per-victim classification can differ. The route's
`scope:result_agg` records PoD's verdict. Payload code retains aggregate
`pod_combat_last_result_agg` as a fallback; packet-specific scripted effects additionally
store `_last_primary_result_agg` and `_last_typed_bonus_result_agg_<k>`.
`push_log` maps these to `entry.var:result_agg` and
`entry.var:typed_bonus_result_agg_<k>`. Each packet selects its own wound
icon, and each typed segment also names its authored class. Retaliation
uses the same result shape for `retal_agg`.

Classify result snapshots from `scope:attack_result_flag`, not damage
magnitude. The dead-target branch can report `hp_damage` with zero
overkill when no empty boxes remained; a magnitude test would incorrectly
relabel it as `immune`.

