## §5 Tier system

Buffs and debuffs to a combatant's **soak** and **hit chance** come in
three sizes. Hit chance is rebuilt from a stored pair base plus its
active tiers. Soak has no combat-side base or recalc; PoD's live
`POD_soak_chance` formula folds the character-side tier variables
directly.

### The three tiers

| Tier | Size | Shipped default | Script value |
|------|------|-----------|--------------|
| 1 | small | ±10 | `pod_combat_tier_small_mag` |
| 2 | medium | ±20 | `pod_combat_tier_medium_mag` |
| 3 | large | ±30 | `pod_combat_tier_large_mag` |

The values in `pod_combat_values.txt` accept debug overrides via
`global_var:pod_combat_tune_tier_*`. Runtime consumers read these values;
tutorial text and this reference must be updated separately when defaults change.

### The eight slots

Each combatant carries up to **eight independent tier values**, one for
every combination of three axes:

- **stat**: `soak` or `hit`
- **direction**: `buff` or `debuff`
- **duration**: `round` (cleared at the holder's own turn boundary, see
  Cleanup) or `combat` (lasts the whole fight)

Each holds `1`, `2`, or `3`, or is absent when that tier is inactive, but
the two stats store them in different places:

- **hit** tiers live on the slot story as
  `slot_hit_<direction>_<duration>_tier`, mirrored into the workspace as
  `pod_combat_<side>_hit_<direction>_<duration>_tier`.
- **soak** tiers live on the COMBATANT as
  `pod_combat_tier_soak_<direction>_<duration>`, with no slot or workspace
  copy. That is the same store `POD_soak_chance` reads, so there is exactly
  one soak tier number per combatant and it is the one the wound roll uses.

### Live hit = base + sum of active tiers (recalc-from-base)

Live hit chance is rebuilt by the recalc effects in
`pod_combat_tier_effects.txt`. They always start from the stored
**base** (`slot_base_hit_chance`, or the workspace
`pod_combat_<side>_base_hit_chance`), then walk the four hit tier
variables and clamp the result. Every tier is a flat percentage point,
**added** for a buff and **subtracted** for a debuff; it clamps to the
live `pod_combat_hit_chance_min` / `_max` values (shipped defaults
`[20, 95]`, debug-tunable).

The output is written back to `slot_hit_chance` (and the workspace
`pod_combat_<side>_hit_chance`). Stealth's hit bonus
(`pod_combat_stealth_hit_bonus`, shipped default +30 and debug-tunable)
folds into the same recalc, keyed off the `*_is_stealthed` flag, so it
survives every rebuild.

Always rebuild hit chance from its base after adding or removing a tier.
Adjusting the clamped total by deltas loses information and causes drift.

Soak tiers are read directly by `POD_soak_chance` (see **Soak bridge to PoD's
wound rolls**). The hit-recalculation scripted effects are:

| Effect | Scope | Rebuilds |
|--------|-------|----------|
| `pod_combat_recalc_hit_actor_effect` / `_hit_target_effect` | workspace | actor / target hit (actor also syncs `pod_combat_current_hit_chance`) |
| `pod_combat_recalc_slot_hit_via_story_effect` | slot story | hit chance on the current slot |

### Upgrade-only application

Applying a tier is **upgrade-only**: a higher tier overwrites a lower
one, but re-applying the same or a lower tier is blocked. Every write in
the tier-application step of `pod_combat_move_effects.txt` is
OR-guarded:

```
OR = {
    NOT = { has_variable = <tier_var> }                  # first application
    var:<tier_var> < $STORY$.var:<move's tier>           # strictly higher
}
```

The redundancy gate rejects a move only when it has no independent useful
payload and all its riders are redundant. Useful hybrids remain selectable;
the inferior tier write does nothing. The write guard also protects console
and test calls. Declaring a tier still triggers hit recalc or soak-stash refresh,
whether the tier increased or not.

Selection-time gates must read the correct store:

- Hit: `pod_combat_tier_upgrade_only_trigger` /
  `pod_combat_slot_tier_rider_upgrades_trigger`, reading workspace/slot fields
  through `pod_combat_session`.
- Soak: `pod_combat_soak_tier_upgrade_only_trigger` /
  `pod_combat_slot_soak_tier_rider_upgrades_trigger`, reading the occupant's
  `pod_combat_tier_soak_*` variables through `pod_combat_<side>_char`.

The caller is the host character, which holds neither store. A wrong-scope
`trigger_if` can skip its test and let an AND gate pass, causing repeated casts.
Use an explicit `trigger_else` where skipping must return false; do not assume
skipped `trigger_if` arms behave identically in AND and OR contexts.
`POD_wound_attacker_forces_agg_trigger` provides an explicit fallback example.

`pod_combat_upgrade_tier_var_effect` requires a strict increase for both
durations. Equal tiers do not refresh: round tiers expire at fixed turn
boundaries and combat tiers at cleanup. AI weights distinguish their value
with `@ai_round_buff_spam` / `@ai_round_buff_lateap` and
`@ai_combat_buff_reward`.

By convention buffs target the actor (`pod_combat_actor_*`) and debuffs
target the victim (`pod_combat_target_*`). A `single_ally` buff or stance
instead targets the selected beneficiary (`pod_combat_target_*`); its
soak tier write and hit recalc follow that ally. PC redundancy gates inspect
the selected ally. The AI chooses its ally after move selection, so its
`single_ally` candidates go to `pod_combat_ai_has_ally_benefiting_trigger`,
which runs the same upgrade tests against each candidate slot's own state.

### Soak bridge to PoD's wound rolls

The combatant's four `pod_combat_tier_soak_<direction>_<duration>` variables
are the sole tier store read by `POD_soak_chance` in `POD_health_track_values.txt`.
Combat writes the tier level and reads PoD's result for display and AI; the
wound pipeline uses the same formula.

- **Buffs multiply remaining damage.** A large buff at the shipped magnitude
  multiplies the unsoaked fraction by 0.7: 64% soak becomes 74.8%, not 94%.
  Its proportional reduction is therefore consistent across existing soak levels.
- **Debuffs subtract percentage points**, allowing them to remove substantial
  protection from high-soak targets.

The buff block converts `soak` to `100 - soak`, applies the factors, then
converts back. Keep it after the flat Fae terms (`POD_quest_of_deed_modifier`,
`cantrip_deja_vu_modifier`) and the 0-100 clamp; moving it into the earlier
product changes the result. Hit tiers remain additive/subtractive percentage
points in `pod_combat_recalc_hit_core_effect`.

### Cleanup

Round-duration tiers expire at each combatant's **own turn boundary**, not at
round end: a buff clears at the start of the holder's turn (so a self soak buff
guards the whole enemy phase, then lapses when the holder acts again); a debuff
at the end of the holder's turn (so it afflicts its victim through that turn).
Combat-duration tiers persist to combat-end cleanup.

| Effect | When | Clears |
|--------|------|--------|
| `pod_combat_clear_actor_round_buffs_effect` | acting combatant's **turn start** | that slot's `hit_buff_round_tier` + the occupant's `pod_combat_tier_soak_buff_round`, then recalcs the slot's hit |
| `pod_combat_clear_actor_round_debuffs_effect` | acting combatant's **turn end** | that slot's `hit_debuff_round_tier` + the occupant's `pod_combat_tier_soak_debuff_round`, then recalcs the slot's hit |
| `pod_combat_clear_combat_tiers_effect` | combat end | all four slot hit tier vars + all four soak tier vars on each occupant |
| `pod_combat_init_slot_from_character_effect` | slot init | the incoming combatant's four soak tier vars (see below) |

The two round clears find the acting slot by the `current_actor` team/position
match (called from the turn-setup / turn-end sites in
`pod_combat_group_effects.txt`); the combat-end clear sweeps every filled slot.
All run in slot-story scope, with `var:slot_char` switching into the participant
character for the soak tiers.

Soak-tier cleanup requires `is_alive`; dead-character variable mutation errors.
`POD_soak_chance` also gates all twelve tier branches on liveness. Slot init
clears surviving variables before a resurrected character enters a new fight.
Do not move cleanup to `POD_on_death`, where the character is already dead.

KO'd living combatants never take another turn, so their round tiers persist
until combat end. They are untargetable and take no wound rolls.

### `team_all`: the both-teams sweep

`targeting = team_all` hits **every living combatant, the caster included**,
on both sides. It uses the same per-victim hit rolls, single stealth
consumption, and per-victim log rows as `team_enemy`.

**The caster is not swept; it is hit actor-side afterwards.** The loop's
filter deliberately skips it:

```
pod_combat_aoe_team_all_sweep_filter_trigger = {
	NOT = { pod_combat_slot_is_current_actor_trigger = yes }
}
```

Loading the caster as both actor and target lets `pod_combat_save_workspace_effect`
overwrite its target-side changes with stale actor-side values. Morale is
affected; HP is character-owned and avoids this conflict. `team_ally` includes
the actor and has the same hazard for morale healing.

The matcher reads `scope:host.var:pod_combat_session`, so save the host before
calling it. Its pointer guard is required in trigger context; see §1's scope
rules.

Each damage or debuff scripted effect iterates over other combatants, then
applies the caster's share actor-side:
typed HP damage through `pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect` (no hit roll
or guard, but ordinary PoD soak/classification), morale damage with `SIDE = actor`,
and debuff tiers through `pod_combat_apply_team_all_self_debuffs_effect`.
Caster HP damage adds to `pod_combat_last_self_damage` alongside any recoil,
and the §16 wrap-up row reports it.

**The caster's share is the move's base `hp_damage` and nothing else.** It is
dealt outside `pod_combat_dispatch_damage_pipeline_effect`, so none of the §6
adders reach it: not the untyped `damage` bonus, not the weapon's bonus damage,
and not the typed damage packets. Only the KIND propagates (weapon retype and a
granted `change_class`). A `team_all` move with a met typed rider therefore
wounds every ally and enemy with the extra packet but not its caster.

`bypass_dodge` / `bypass_stealth` are not self-applied. They govern contact
with other victims; consuming caster stealth early would remove the burst's
stealth accuracy bonus.

Other rules:

- **Damage and debuffs broadcast; support does not.** §5b broadcasts tier
  debuffs and stance-strips across the sweep, but §5c/§9/§10/§12/§12b route
  buffs, heals and beneficiary stances to the **actor** for every mode
  except `single_ally` / `single_pc`. A `team_all` heal would therefore mend
  only its caster, so that combination is never legal: pair a support
  payload with `team_ally`, never `team_all`.
- **No reactions.** §15 skips `team_all` for the same reason it skips
  `team_enemy`: a counter can only fire from one workspace target, so firing
  would arbitrarily single out the pre-AoE one.
- **Victory is scored after the move, not mid-sweep.** A burst that KOs the
  last enemy keeps sweeping the remaining allies. The ally-down morale shock
  fires per KO inside the loop but is floored at 1 morale, so it can never
  cascade a second KO.

AI weighting combines the enemy-count reward `@ai_aoe_multi_enemy` with
`@ai_aoe_ff_self` on every cast and the additional friendly-fire penalties
`@ai_aoe_ff_one_ally` / `@ai_aoe_ff_two_ally` while teammates remain.

