## §6 Reaction system

A **reaction** is armed by a move and fires on the next eligible single-target
HP-damage attack. Pure morale/control/utility moves and `team_enemy` /
`team_all` attacks do not check reactions.

- **Thorns:** contact (`hp_damage`, `soaked`, `guard_blocked`).
- **Riposte:** evasion (`missed`, `dodged`, `stealthed`).
- **Always:** any attack outcome, including `immune`.

At most one counter fires per attack. A hit/miss charge is checked before
Always; a resolved specific counter preserves Always for a later attack.
Replacement compares damage only within the same family; the three charges
can coexist.

### Arming (during the arming combatant's own move)

In the move-execution step that handles retaliation stances
(`pod_combat_move_effects.txt`), a move with `retaliate_if_hit`,
`retaliate_if_missed`, or `retaliate_if_attacked` writes the armed
reaction onto the actor's workspace, which is mirrored onto its slot
story:

| Move var | Workspace var armed | Fires on |
|----------|--------------------|----------|
| `retaliate_if_hit` | `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_hit` | contact |
| `retaliate_if_missed` | `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_miss` | evasion |
| `retaliate_if_attacked` | `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_any` | any resolved attack |

The candidate counter damage is chosen as `retaliate_damage` →
`hp_damage` → `1`; an arming move therefore always supplies an amount.
Arming is take-strongest: the candidate replaces an existing reaction
only when its amount is strictly larger. An equal or smaller candidate
leaves both the amount and its current kind unchanged. Three companion
vars describe each `_hit` / `_miss` / `_any` counter:

| Companion var | Meaning |
|---------------|---------|
| `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_<hit\|miss\|any>_kind` | counter's damage kind: `morale`, `aggravated`, or an elemental class flag (fire/cold/…); unset = plain |
| `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_<hit\|miss\|any>_kind_from_weapon` | weapon- or `change_class`-derived kind; enables the fire-time immunity fallback and mirrors to the slot |
| `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_<hit\|miss\|any>_morale` | presence companion to a `morale` kind (drives the slot tooltip) |

Kind precedence is **morale > `retaliate_agg` > move element > granted
`change_class` > weapon retype > unset**. `pod_combat_seed_kind_from_class_effect`
seeds the authored class using literal flags. Both `change_class` and weapon
retype also set `_kind_from_weapon` for the fire-time immunity fallback.

**Weapon- and `change_class`-derived kinds fall back to plain damage against immunity.**
At fire time the live weapon profile belongs to the attacker, so
`pod_combat_stamped_type_is_wasted_trigger` checks the counter's saved kind.
If its victim is immune, it removes the kind and `_kind_from_weapon` marker.
Authored elemental counters retain their type and may deal no damage.
An armed tooltip can therefore show an element that will not be used against
a particular victim.

The slot story carries the same fields as `slot_retaliate_hit` /
`slot_retaliate_miss` / `slot_retaliate_any` (+ the `_kind` / `_kind_from_weapon` / `_morale`
companions), which drive the per-slot retaliation status icon
(`pod_combat_slot_has_retaliation_value`) and its tooltip
(`PODCombatStatusRetaliationText`).

### Firing (during the next attacker's move)

For a non-`team_enemy`, non-`team_all` move carrying `hp_damage`, the
reaction phase calls `pod_combat_check_retaliation_effect` with
`TARGET_SIDE` = the side holding the stance (usually `target`) and
`ACTOR_SIDE` = the attacker who will take the counter. It checks the
three armed slots **in this order**:

| Armed var | Gated by | Fires on |
|-----------|----------|----------|
| `pod_combat_<side>_retaliate_hit` | `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_contact_trigger` | `hp_damage` / `soaked` / `guard_blocked` |
| `pod_combat_<side>_retaliate_miss` | `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_miss_trigger` | `missed` / `dodged` / `stealthed` |
| `pod_combat_<side>_retaliate_any` | `pod_combat_reaction_any_condition_trigger` | any resolved attack the two above did not answer |

The first two gates read `pod_combat_last_outcome` /
`pod_combat_last_attack_result` (§4). The Always gate
combines `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_attack_trigger` with absence of
`pod_combat_last_retal_fired`; changing the call order changes which charge is
consumed. `pod_combat_apply_reaction_if_triggered_effect` tests, fires, then
clears the charge and its companions. A matching attack consumes the charge
even when the counter's spirit gate prevents damage. That failure does not set
`pod_combat_last_retal_fired`, so an armed Always charge can also be checked
and consumed by the same blocked attack.

### How the counter resolves

`pod_combat_apply_retaliation_effect` skips contact resolution, hit rolls,
and guard. It checks spirit eligibility before dispatch: a counter cannot harm
an incorporeal spirit unless the retaliator can damage spirits. Failure leaves
the main attack's log result intact.

Kind dispatch order is **morale > typed > plain**:

| Branch | Effect | Damage routing |
|--------|--------|----------------|
| morale (`_kind` = `morale`) | erodes `pod_combat_<actor>_morale`, capped at current morale; does **not** consume the victim's stance | logs the actual morale lost; sets `pod_combat_last_retal_morale` |
| typed (`_kind` = `aggravated` or an elemental class) | `pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect` maps the class to a PoD `death_reason` (`aggravated` → `duel_aggravated`, `holy` → `true_faith`; the same kind table as the direct-hit and DoT switches) and deals it bypass-guard | sets `pod_combat_last_retal_agg` from the damage-routing scripted effect's actual `scope:result_agg` (PoD's per-victim classification), not the armed class |
| plain (`_kind` unset) | bypass-guard HP via `REASON = duel` | — |

Each branch records `pod_combat_last_retal_damage` (post-soak HP or capped
morale loss), then calls `pod_combat_set_retal_fired_effect`. The independent
`pod_combat_last_retal_fired` field adds the counter rider without replacing
the main attack result. See **Combat log**.

