## §7 Engine constraints

These constraints affect combat's slot loops, workspace, and GUI renderers.
Several failures produce missing or stale state without an obvious error.

### Var mutation on dead characters errors

Guard character cleanup with `is_alive = yes`: `remove_variable` /
`clear_variable_list` error on dead or removed characters ("scope does not have
variables"). Reads such as `is_alive` and `is_ai` still resolve on dead-but-present
characters. KO'd living participants still require ordinary per-fight cleanup.

### The action workspace is a persistent current-pair cache

The workspace variables (`pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*`
on the fight story) hold the currently loaded actor and target. A full
load establishes the pair at turn setup; target-only loads repoint it
for swaps and AoE iterations. A typical turn looks like:

```
pod_combat_load_workspace_effect       ← establish actor + target
    (one or more actions; target may be reloaded between them)
pod_combat_save_workspace_effect       ← write mirrors back
    (workspace remains loaded and may serve the next action)
```

`pod_combat_save_workspace_effect` leaves the pair loaded. PC buttons can
execute and save repeatedly; AI changes reload it as needed. Read non-loaded
combatants directly from their slots. See **Where state lives** for ownership.

**Direct writes to loaded slots must update the workspace too.** Otherwise the
next save overwrites the changed slot field with its stale workspace mirror.
`pod_combat_apply_ally_down_shock_to_slot_effect` updates actor/target morale
when `pod_combat_slot_is_current_actor_trigger` /
`pod_combat_slot_is_selected_target_trigger` identifies the affected slot.

**AoE tails reload only the target** with
`pod_combat_reload_selected_target_effect`. A full reload would discard actor
changes made during the sweep. Target load plus `recalc_pair_hit_chance` restores
the selected pair's hit chance without reloading the actor.

Enemy HP AoE uses `pod_combat_aoe_suppress_stealth_consume` to defer stealth
consumption until after the loop, giving each victim the same stealth hit bonus.

Keep mirrored fields paired in the load/save scripted effects. There is no runtime
workspace-sync checker; see §11 for adding a mirror field.

### Scope traps inside slot loops

**Push system logs from the host.** `pod_combat_push_system_log_effect` and
`_named_effect` save their caller as `combat_host`. Calling from `var:slot_char`
replaces that shared scope with the participant and breaks later AI matchers.
Save the host before iterating and call through it, as
`pod_combat_apply_delirium_to_victim_effect` does.

The following rules apply inside `pod_combat_active_slots` and DoT iterators.

**Finish inline slot reads/writes before switching scope.** After inline
`scope:X = { }` or `var:slot_char = { }` switches, later inline statements can
read the iterator's variables as unset. Scripted-effect calls still read `this`
correctly. AoE soak writes through `var:slot_char` therefore come last.

HP damage uses a workspace round-trip: load target from slot, call the damage
scripted effect through `scope:host`, save target, then check KO. Do not deal wounds from
slot scope (see **Damage pipeline**).

**Do not lift a flag-valued variable into a scope value.**
`save_scope_value_as` converts that path to a plain value; later `= flag:X`
comparisons produce type errors. A trigger and its negation can both fail.

Safe paths:

- Variable-to-variable copies preserve flags:
  `set_variable = { name = A value = var:B }`, including object dereferences
  such as `value = scope:slot.var:combat_team`.
  `pod_combat_find_next_actor_effect` copies `combat_team` through
  `pod_combat_best_team` to `pod_combat_current_actor_team`, preserving its
  type for later team comparisons.
- Saved object scopes allow direct comparisons:
  `scope:obj.var:flag_var = flag:X`.
- Literal flags remain typed in variables and saved scope values. For example,
  `pod_combat_seed_kind_from_class_effect` seeds `flag:duel`, then compares the
  source and writes the appropriate literal. Duel-rule flags also use literals
  on the shell card (§10).
- `switch` and `if` comparisons of the source variable do not copy it. The DoT and
  retaliation scripted effects and the move-unlock scripted triggers use these.
- Numeric values such as `dot_amount` / `dot_rounds` can be lifted normally.

**A `var:X` read errors when `X` is unset.** Reading
`var:slot_dot_rounds` (etc.) on a slot that has no such variable raises
an error, not a default. Guard every potentially-absent read with
`has_variable` (or filter the iteration `limit` on it). Many slot fields
are deliberately absence-as-signal (`slot_init_bonus`, `slot_ap_drain`,
`slot_dot_*`, the tier vars, stance flags) — see **Slot lifecycle** for
the full list.

### Character stats stay authoritative; current-pair reads may be hoisted

Read health through the slot HP values, which resolve the participant's
`POD_empty_damage_boxes`, `POD_total_damage_boxes`, and
`POD_health_aggravated_damage_num`. The character wound track is authoritative;
slot logic must not maintain a separate HP total. See **Variable reference**
for the HP values and **Damage pipeline** for writes through `POD_increase_wounds_*`.

**Soak and prowess remain character-authoritative but are hoisted for
the loaded pair.** `pod_combat_stash_side_live_stats_effect` samples
`POD_soak_chance` and prowess when a side loads, then AI scoring and
workspace consumers reuse those values until the next refresh. Soak is
also refreshed after a move writes a soak tier, and the actual PoD wound
call evaluates its own authoritative soak path. This keeps wound-track
prowess penalties and live soak changes feeding the fight without
re-walking expensive script values for every candidate move.

**The combat window pins the game clock**, so no daily skill recalculation
ever lands during a fight. Any code that adds or removes a character
modifier mid-fight must therefore call `force_character_skill_recalculation`
on that character itself, or no `prowess` read - the stash, the hit
formula, initiative, auto-resolve, the slot card - will ever see it.

The **wound-bar display mirror** (`slot_mirror_total` / `slot_mirror_agg` /
`slot_mirror_maxhp`) supplies the GUI's `GetSegmentedProgressTargetBar` wound
bars, avoiding repeated evaluation of the nested wound formulas. Game logic and AI
continue reading authoritative character health.

At workspace save, `pod_combat_refresh_changed_hp_mirrors_effect` samples each
slot's current maximum HP, raw superficial/aggravated counters (absent = zero),
and character liveness. It rebuilds the mirror only when these inputs differ
from its saved `slot_mirror_raw_sup`, `slot_mirror_raw_agg`, `slot_mirror_alive`
and `slot_mirror_maxhp`, or any mirror/snapshot variable is missing. This catches
external healing, aggravated-to-superficial conversion, death and maximum-HP
changes at the same post-action boundary without adding mutation hooks.

Maximum HP must be sampled even when wound counters are unchanged because
perks, splats, and modifiers also affect it. Rebuilds use the canonical wound
values' clamps, floors, and dead-character full bars. Do not round or clamp raw
input snapshots; extend the comparison if the wound formula gains inputs.

Slot initialization, round-end DoT, and auto-resolve fully refresh the mirrors
and seed their input snapshots. Missing snapshots force refresh. GUI health
updates remain boundary-based; do not bind individual wound pips to live formulas.

The **portrait form cache** stores PoD's two umbrella form checks in
`pod_combat_form_*` character flags. Slot initialization seeds it; completed
shapeshifts refresh the affected participant. Shared form effects and Golconda
transitions invalidate it before changing inputs. Until refresh, portrait
scripted triggers evaluate the form conditions directly. Turn setup refreshes invalid caches; HP refresh
does not evaluate forms. Both teardown paths clear the flags, with a three-day
expiry as fallback cleanup.

New effects changing portrait inputs during combat must invalidate before
mutation and may refresh afterward with `pod_combat_refresh_char_form_cache_effect`.

### DoT ticks at round end and must respect the slot-loop rules

A burn (`slot_dot_amount` / `slot_dot_rounds` / `slot_dot_kind` /
`slot_dot_source`) ticks once per round in
`pod_combat_tick_dots_effect`, called from
`pod_combat_group_end_round_effect` after the round increment and before
reinforcement seating / initiative recalc. The tick loop body is ordered
into phases precisely to obey the scope-trap rules above:

1. **Read and update the slot:** lift tick amount, save the victim, resolve
   `scope:dot_attacker` from living `slot_dot_source` (otherwise the victim),
   and decrement `slot_dot_rounds`. Complete these before inline scope switches.
2. **Apply damage:** morale ticks write `slot_morale`; HP ticks load the target,
   call `pod_combat_deal_dot_tick_from_host_effect` through `scope:host`, save,
   and check KO. Kind dispatch supplies `REASON` and
   `ATTACKER = scope:dot_attacker`. Both paths return applied damage in
   `scope:dot_boxes_applied`; HP also returns `scope:dot_result_agg` and
   `scope:dot_result_immune`.
3. **Log the result:** `pod_combat_push_dot_tick_resolved_log_effect` uses those
   saved results after damage, not the authored amount/class. Hard immunity has
   its own row; other zero-damage results, including clay-man protection, use
   blocked text rather than Soak. This contained call remains safe after the
   preceding scope switches.
4. **Expire:** `pod_combat_expire_dot_if_done_effect` removes all four DoT
   variables at rounds ≤ 0. Keep the kind until after wound dispatch.

DoT ticks are **unsoakable by design**
(`pod_combat_apply_dot_wound_effect` mirrors PoD's wound effect minus
the soak roll); splat-weakness multiplier, damage cap, hard immunity,
and clay-man protection still apply. See **Damage pipeline** for the
wound routing.

### customizable_loc raw_text evaluates on every row

Combat-log `raw_text` evaluates on every row even when its widget is hidden.
System, DoT, and mind-control rows may lack `move` or the expected entry variables.

Start each customizable localization with a blank/neutral branch for missing required scopes or
variables, before any comparison. `PODCombatLogSeg<X>` and
`PODCombatLogResultText` follow this rule. Widget visibility alone cannot protect
a read such as `scope:move.var:hp_damage`.

### Combat move-button validation

`pod_combat_execute_move.is_valid` gates active combat before reading turn
ownership, then gates an unfinished fight and the clicking client's turn,
then the controlled body's KO status, then the move and actor scopes. Only
that successful path enters `POD_can_execute_combat_move_trigger`. Each failed
branch returns false explicitly; a skipped `trigger_if` alone would pass.
The expensive move checks therefore do not run while the player cannot act.
Root remains the clicking client and the actor remains the driven combatant.
The KO gate applies only to move commands: End Turn must remain available.

All four button categories need explicit validity bindings for enabled state,
background grayscale, and gray/colored labels. Native disabled rendering does
not supply the required appearance. No validity state is stored on characters
or moves.

For profiling, compare equal-duration captures with the same loadout and
separate hovered/unhovered runs. Multiple bindings do not prove independent
engine evaluations.

### Move tooltip variants

The move card (`pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip`, declared in
`pod_combat_moves_window.gui`) renders title / effects / flavor and ends
in an empty `block "additional_text" {}`. Three variants exist, and a
surface picks one by which type it names:

| Variant | Type | Surfaces |
|---|---|---|
| Base | `pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip` | combat-log rows, perk / inline-loc links |
| Eligibility | `pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip_editor` | loadout editor: equipped column, compact pool card, sheet name cell (the detailed pool card has no tooltipwidget by design and carries the same two sections inline) |
| Usability | `pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip_live` | the four combat-bar move buttons |

Both non-base variants fill `additional_text` with failures-only reason
lines. Nothing renders when the move is usable, so the base card is what
a valid move shows in every variant.

The reason text comes from render-only scripted GUIs -
`pod_combat_move_usability_tt` and `pod_combat_loadout_block_tt` - whose
`is_valid` exists purely to be rendered by `ScriptedGui.IsValidTooltip`.
Never bind `IsValid` / `Execute` to one: they have no effect block, and
their answer is deliberately narrower than the real gate's. Each clause is

```
trigger_if = {
    limit = { <applicability>  NOT = { <shared predicate> } }
    custom_tooltip = { text = <key>  always = no }
}
```

This suppresses passing conditions. Otherwise `IsValidTooltip` renders both
passes and failures, making failure-phrased text such as "Select an enemy target"
appear as a successful condition. The editor's unlock requirement display uses
`is_valid_showing_failures_only` for the same purpose.

The button gate and its reason line share scripted triggers in
`pod_combat_move_execution_triggers.txt` (`SHARED USABILITY TRIGGERS`) and
`pod_combat_triggers.txt` (capacity/control-slot scripted triggers). A new blocking rule
needs all three: a shared scripted trigger, its call in the gate, and a matching
`_tt` SGUI reason.

Tooltip SGUI calls run in a `tooltipwidget` subtree built on hover. They are
separate from button-validity bindings. The editor's per-row `enabled` binding
calls `pod_combat_loadout_room`, which reads cached counts and membership,
not the unlock scripted trigger.

### An open event keeps its scope table; trigger_event copies it inline

The duel shell (§10) relies on two behaviors tested in-game:

- **A held-open event preserves its saved-scope table across save/reload.**
  Character, artifact, title, numeric, and flag scope values remain available
  when its option is clicked; flag values still compare to literals.
- **`trigger_event` copies the table at the call site.** Scopes saved afterward
  are not visible to the fired event. The builder therefore opens the shell
  before saving its internal scopes.

To test these contracts, save each scope type in an event, fire another event,
and read them from its option; repeat across save/reload.

Neither result should be assumed to extend to `save_temporary_scope_as`
(documented as same-effect only) or to scopes saved inside a
scripted-GUI execution, which have no chain to persist into.

