## §8 Combat end

Victory checks and auto-resolve set `pod_combat_winner`,
`pod_combat_loser`, and `pod_combat_is_over`. The player then calls
`pod_combat_end_combat_effect` through the duel builder's shell card, or
**End Combat** for a fight without that card.

The combat window has no close button
(`blockoverride "button_close" { visible = no }`): abandoning a duel
would leave its launching event waiting for a follow-up. Auto-resolve
provides a way to finish without playing the remaining turns.

If the host dies externally during an active fight, `POD_on_death`
(`common/on_action/POD_vanilla_death.txt`) calls
`pod_combat_host_died_release_effect`. Its guard checks that the session
is active and its `ctx_host` is the dying character. The release mutates
only living characters and stories: it removes engaged flags and duel
modifiers, clears the `POD_soak_chance` tier bridge, AI scratch and
pending reinforcements,
despawns summons, and ends the Charon-owned fight story last. The shared
scripted effects `pod_combat_clear_combat_tiers_effect`,
`pod_combat_clear_all_ai_scratch_effect`, and
`pod_combat_strip_pending_reinforcement_vars_effect` also serve normal
cleanup. Ending the fight cascades its slots and log rows; the dead
host's character-side pointers remain inert in the save.

This release does not derive an outcome or fire the original follow-up.
A surviving shell card closes through its no-outcome option. Recovery of
the parent event chain after an external host death is unsupported.
`.9999` phase 6 also uses this release when phase 4 kills the host; a
living host receives `pod_combat_cleanup_effect` instead.

### Victory check

`pod_combat_check_group_victory_effect` runs at action boundaries
(workspace save, end of each AI turn, after a DoT tick), not per damage
event. This prevents an AoE from resolving victory separately for each
victim. First match wins:

| # | Condition | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | No alive enemy slot | player wins; loser = enemy leader (enemy/0) |
| 2 | PC slot (player/0) KO'd | player loses; winner = lowest-position living enemy (enemy leader only as fallback) |
| 3 | No alive player-team slot | player loses; winner = lowest-position living enemy (covers a bypassed PC death check) |

A match sets the outcome variables, saves `scope:pod_combat_target`, and
pushes the victory/defeat log row. The target is the defeated enemy leader
on a player win, or the living enemy selected by
`pod_combat_save_enemy_victor_effect` on a loss. The window remains open
until the player concludes (§10).

### Auto-resolve

`pod_combat_auto_resolve_effect` guards against re-entry with
`pod_combat_is_over`, then calls `pod_combat_auto_resolve_run_effect`.
The effect-level guard prevents a second click from rerolling the outcome
or applying wounds before the GUI updates its own `is_valid`.

The roll sums living occupants' live prowess per team, flooring each
contribution at 0 to keep `integer_range` valid. Each team scores
`sum + rand(0..sum)`; the higher score wins, with ties favoring the player.
Wound penalties therefore affect the roll.

**The spirit-gate verdict runs first.** A side that cannot damage a
required incorporeal opponent loses without a prowess roll (§6):

* `pod_combat_slot_has_spirit_answer_trigger` (slot scope) accepts
  `slot_can_damage_spirit`, an equipped ungated `hp_damage` move, an
  equipped ungated HP DoT, or a permitted friendly spirit-family summon.
  Exclude `dot_morale`: `dot_amount` covers both HP and morale DoTs.
  Spirit summons qualify through the summoned character's capability;
  `controlled_animal` does not. The summon branch bypasses
  `checks_spirit_gate`, which those self-targeting moves carry without
  entering contact resolution. It reads the live `pod_combat_loadout`
  because AI loadouts are seeded after slot initialization. Duels that
  bar summoning disable this branch. Morale offense does not qualify.
* `pod_combat_slot_has_live_hp_burn_trigger` exempts an opponent with
  an active HP burn. Its ticks can still cause a KO after the caster
  dies, re-attributing to the victim.
* **Limitation:** the answer test does not check the blocking spirit's
  immunity to the credited damage class. A sun-only loadout can qualify
  and permit the roll despite being ineffective against that spirit.
  This can miss a forced verdict, but does not force an incorrect loss.
* `pod_combat_player_side_cannot_win_trigger` and
  `pod_combat_enemy_side_cannot_win_trigger` (host scope) use the same
  team/liveness scripted triggers as the prowess loops. Their victory conditions
  differ: one undamageable enemy prevents a player win, while only an
  undamageable **PC** prevents an enemy win. An incorporeal player ally
  does not protect a corporeal PC.

Each verdict branch excludes the opposing verdict. If both report
incapacity, the effect rolls instead; normally an incorporeal combatant
itself passes `POD_can_naturally_damage_spirit_trigger`. Missing spirit
slot flags also leave the roll available.

Auto-resolve stays enabled under a forced loss so an unwinnable fight
can end. `pod_combat_auto_resolve_desc` appends
`PODCombatAutoResolveVerdict`: a loss warning, a win notice, or blank.

Both the verdict and roll use `pod_combat_set_outcome_pair_effect`, which
writes both outcome variables in every branch. The post-combat consumer
requires both; an incomplete pair becomes a draw. A player win records
the enemy leader as loser. An enemy win uses
`pod_combat_save_enemy_victor_effect` to select the lowest-position living
enemy, falling back to the leader only if all enemies are KO'd.
`pod_combat_zero_loser_leader_morale_effect` then zeroes the losing
leader's morale, and the effect pushes the outcome row.

#### The final scramble

`pod_combat_auto_resolve_apply_wounds_effect` applies 0-4 pre-soak wound
boxes to each combatant still standing, including winners. It excludes
summons, which cleanup despawns, and clones (`POD_is_clone_trigger`),
whose death check bypasses
`POD_can_die_from_wounds` and could kill them inline despite the gate.

* Loser/winner base wounds are 2/2 in a close fight, 3/1 when the winning
  score reaches 1.5x the loser's, and 4/0 at 2.5x. They sum to
  `pod_combat_auto_resolve_wound_max`. Equal prowess sums can reach the
  1.5x band but not 2.5x. Comparisons avoid division, so two zero scores
  count as close.
* Each combatant adds jitter (`-1..+1`), clamped to 0-4.
* A spirit-gate verdict assigns base 4 to the losing side and 0 to the
  side it could not damage.
* Practice skips the pass: restoring wound boxes would not undo toasts,
  trait XP or treatment hooks.

Wounds use `POD_increase_wounds_hostile_effect` with `REASON = duel`,
including immunity and soak. The credited attacker is the opposing
outcome principal, guarded alive with the death sweep's `trigger_if`
pattern. Preserve these constraints:

1. Save `scope:combat_host` before the wound call, as the run effect does.
   Without it, `POD_increase_wounds_effect` uses out-of-combat artifact
   soak shred and
   elemental/bane-weapon classification. Attacker body powers (infernal
   glory, plague-crow talons, archid strength) can still aggravate damage.
2. Skip zero-count calls. Immunity and Clay Man checks precede the amount
   check, so zero wounds can still produce toasts or consume an amulet.
3. Log the change in `POD_total_damage_boxes` and
   `POD_aggravated_damage_boxes`; use the aggravated key if any aggravated
   box landed. `scope:landed_damage` describes a packet without a wound
   class, while body powers and overflow can change the boxes applied.
4. Keep one contained call per slot iteration (§7, *Scope traps inside
   slot loops*). Enter `var:slot_char` only after the last slot-field
   read. `pod_combat_push_auto_resolve_log_effect` reads the saved
   `scope:combat_host`; the system-log scripted effect would replace it with the
   calling slot story.

The pass does not mark KOs or spill damage into morale. The outcome is
already fixed, and `pod_combat_mark_slot_ko_effect` would add ally-down
shocks and Mental Strain. Health
finalization, the death sweep and strain read character life state, not
`slot_alive`.

The active death gate suppresses inline deaths. `fatality = no` caps
wounds and de-escalates aggravated boxes afterwards; fatal modes retain
wounds, including aggravated overflow, for deferred death resolution.

### Ending the combat

`pod_combat_end_combat_effect` guards on the fight's `pod_combat_active`.
Checking only the character's session pointer would allow re-entry after
the handoff clears active but retains the pointer. It runs:

1. **Health finalization and death-gate removal**
   (`pod_combat_finalize_combat_health_effect`). For `fatality = no`,
   leave one empty box (remove superficial first, otherwise aggravated)
   and convert aggravated wounds below the death threshold. For
   `practice`, restore the initialization snapshot with
   `pod_combat_restore_practice_wounds_effect`. Fatal modes keep wounds.
   Then remove `pod_combat_nonfatal_active`. Do not resolve deaths here:
   killing the host would prevent `trigger_event` from reaching `.9999`,
   losing the follow-up and teardown. Deaths resolve during writeback.
2. **Morale to Mental Strain** (`pod_combat_apply_morale_strain_effect`),
   skipped for practice. Each living combatant receives
   `POD_static_stress_gain` from
   `(pod_combat_slot_max_morale_value - slot_morale) *
   pod_combat_morale_stress_multiplier` (default multiplier 3). Apply it
   once per combatant because each call recalculates skills.
3. Save winner/loser scopes and call `pod_combat_duel_end_effect` while
   slot variables remain available. It removes discipline battle
   modifiers and the `pod_combat_duel_active` marker.
4. If the fight carries `follow_up`, remove `pod_combat_active`, retain
   `pod_combat_is_over`, and fire `pod_combat.9999`. Otherwise log the
   missing handoff, show a victory/defeat toast if a winner is known, and
   call `pod_combat_cleanup_effect`. Every shipped launcher supplies
   `follow_up`; the standalone branch is defensive.

### Post-combat handoff

`POD_combat_create_duel_effect` opens `pod_combat.9995` without delay
inside the launching event chain. The shell retains the caller's saved
scopes, including `sc_*` principals, rule flags and parent-specific
aliases. Its **Conclude** option runs writeback inside that chain, so
`.9999` and the follow-up inherit those scopes. A scripted-GUI click
cannot preserve that table (§7).

`gui/event_windows/pod_combat_shell.gui` displays the card only after the
fight ends. The event must open at launch to capture the table; deferring
its creation until a GUI click would lose it.

Before opening the shell, the builder saves canonical `sc_initiator`,
`sc_attacker`, `sc_defender`, `fatality`, `fixed`, and `locale` scopes from
its parameters. Open the shell before subsequent builder scripted effects:
`pod_combat_calc_turn_orders_effect`, for example, writes `scope:host`
and `scope:me`, potentially replacing the parent's aliases. Set
`pod_combat_sc_shell` only after fight creation, since stale-fight cleanup
would otherwise remove it.

`pod_combat.9999` runs these phases:

1. Bind `scope:sc_ctx` to the host's `pod_combat_session` using `?=`.
   This is the fight story, including the follow-up ID.
2. **Fallback if `scope:sc_attacker` is absent.**
   `POD_combat_restore_sc_context_effect` and
   `POD_combat_restore_sc_rule_flags_effect` restore principals and rule
   flags from the fight. They also supply attacker aliases `attacker` /
   `challenger` and defender aliases `victim` / `recipient` / `defender` /
   `target` / `adversary`. Parent-specific aliases cannot be recovered.
   This supports dispatch without a shell; if `pod_combat_sc_shell` is
   set, the missing table produces an `error_log`.
3. **Derive the outcome** (`POD_combat_derive_sc_outcome_effect`):
   `sc_victor`, `sc_loser`, the victor snap, `sc_finished` and
   `victory_type`. Run after fallback so the snap can use restored
   principals.
4. **Finalize the duel** (`POD_combat_apply_duel_finalization_effect`):
   release principals' `engaged_in_single_combat`, increment one equipped
   primary weapon's `artifact_duel_var` for the victor, and award Fera
   ferocity renown outside practice. Guaranteed-kill modes
   (`fatality = always`, or `default` with tribal governments) call
   `pod_death` on a living loser before
   `pod_combat_resolve_suppressed_deaths_effect` handles deferred wound
   deaths. `practice` and `no` skip deaths.
5. Fire `OUTPUT_EVENT`, with the vanilla initiator → defender → attacker
   fallback.
6. **Cleanup.** For a living host, `pod_combat_cleanup_effect` clears soak
   tiers and the weapon profile, calls `pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect`
   to remove reinforcements, AI scratch and summons, then ends the fight
   story and removes the session pointer. A dead host uses the release
   described above. End the fight **last**: its cascade ends all slots
   and log rows and discards workspace, counters, outcomes, rule flags,
   `pod_combat_step_mode`, `sc_*` principals and follow-up ID. Do not add
   separate clears or slot/log teardown loops.
   `pod_combat_turn_order_list` is excluded from the cascade because it
   references the same slots in another order.

#### Deferred death resolution

Every fatality mode uses `pod_combat_uses_death_gate_trigger` to suppress
PoD's inline `POD_health_death_check_effect` (via
`POD_apply_wound_boxes`) during combat. Otherwise,
characters with guaranteed resurrection could run
`pod_resurrection_effect` → `POD_full_heal_effect` mid-action, preventing
a KO and making the hit log as soaked. This includes wraiths, spectres,
hsien, siochain, `extra_life_*`, and phoenix/scarab/rebirthing perks.

A full wound track instead KOs the slot through
`POD_total_damage_boxes >= POD_health_track_length`. After health
finalization removes the gate, deaths resolve once during writeback,
consistent with PoD's `work_out_wounds_or_death_effect` in
`events/single_combat_events.txt`.

`pod_combat_resolve_suppressed_deaths_effect` iterates
`pod_combat_active_slots` on `scope:sc_ctx`, which remains valid if the
host dies. It calls `POD_health_death_check_effect` once per surviving
combatant, preserving the full-track,
`POD_aggravated_damage_threshold_for_death` and
`POD_can_die_from_wounds` checks. Superficial-only KOs survive; immortals
without guaranteed resurrection can use `POD_long_depose_effect`.

The sweep follows three rules:

* Skip `fatality = no` and `practice` through
  `pod_combat_duel_is_bloodless_trigger`. The same trigger selects the
  CERTÁMEN title; fatal mage duels use DUEL.
* Skip summons through `pod_combat_slot_char_is_summon_trigger`, reading
  the fight's `pod_combat_summoned_chars`. Cleanup uses
  `death_disappearance` so no corpse remains.
* Credit the opposing principal. Capture
  `scope:pod_combat_player_principal` and `scope:pod_combat_enemy_victor`
  before forced deaths, since their savers read through the host. Skip
  victims whose opposing principal is missing or dead rather than pass
  a dead killer to `POD_commit_murder_effect`; the next
  `POD_refresh_health_track` settles their wounds.

A wraith may resurrect after writeback because the duel has resolved.
For Umbra encounters, `POD_umbra_lost.100` removes the resulting spectre
the next day.

The shell card is the only conclusion control for builder-started
fights. **End Combat** is hidden while
`PODCombatFight.Var('pod_combat_sc_shell').IsSet`. Concluding through the
GUI would lose scopes and leave the card open. The option uses
`pod_combat_shell_owns_live_fight_trigger` to match the captured
principals against the live fight; it does not compare unique fight IDs.
When no fight is live, the option closes the card without further action.

